# MECON USB Connector: udev rule TEMPLATE. It ships no real ids and does nothing until you edit it.
#
# 1. Find your radio's ids:   udevadm info -a -n /dev/ttyACM0 | grep -E 'idVendor|idProduct|ATTRS\{serial\}'
#    or                       mecon-connector --list-ports
# 2. Uncomment the line below and replace VID, PID and SERIAL with those values. They must be the
#    same ones as device.select in /etc/mecon-connector/config.toml (vid, pid, usb_serial).
# 3. sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=tty
#
# What it does for the matched device only:
#   GROUP/MODE  the node is owned by group dialout, mode 0660 (the service runs with that group);
#   SYMLINK     a fixed name /dev/mecon/connector-radio for people and scripts. The Connector itself
#               selects by /dev/serial/by-id (made by the stock rules from vendor, product and serial)
#               or by vid/pid, never by this link or by /dev/ttyACM0;
#   ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE  keeps ModemManager from probing the port with AT commands, which would
#               corrupt the framing and can reset the board.
#
# A radio whose USB descriptor has no serial number cannot be told apart from a second identical
# one: leave ATTRS{serial} out only if exactly one such device is ever attached.
#
# SUBSYSTEM=="tty", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="VID", ATTRS{idProduct}=="PID", ATTRS{serial}=="SERIAL", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", SYMLINK+="mecon/connector-radio", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1"
#
# A bench host that names its ports explicitly (device.select.path, or --port on the command line) can use
# one link per board instead of a by-id pattern: give the rule above SYMLINK+="bench/NAME" (NAME made up per
# board) and select path = "/dev/bench/NAME". The link must be stable across replugs; a kernel node
# (/dev/ttyACM0) is refused.
#
# With [dfu] enabled, the board's bootloader enumerates as another USB device (other ids, often another serial
# number). Give it a rule of its own the same way (ids read with --list-ports while the board is in its
# bootloader), with its own SYMLINK name if you select by path.
